Z||000000||Scott2M4||12-06-1999||11:01 AM||||I know it wasn't a Fiero chassis but I figured one of you would know this.<P>Remember the Ferrari Daytona in the television show Miami Vice?  I've heard it was a kit car.  If so, what chassis was used?||||reg||5
Z||000001||peterh||12-06-1999||11:14 AM||||I heard it was based on a corvette.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||william_thorniii||12-06-1999||12:14 PM||||I don't know the validity of this, but I had heard that the Ferrari 308 used the the Magnum P.I. series was only a 308 body to reduce costs.  Supposedly, to make it sound real, they rented a 308 for a week to tape the way it sounded and then dubbed them in during the editing of the show.  Again, I do not know what chassis was under there.  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||deceler8||12-06-1999||02:31 PM||||Another little known fact is that Magnum was played not by Tom Selleck, but rather a "rebodied" Don Knotts.||||reg||6
Z||000004||mrfixit58||12-06-1999||04:08 PM||||The Daytona in Miami Vice was a kit from a 70's vintage Corvette. The company that produced the kit is no longer in business. The molds have been sold and there have been a few attemps to re-offer this kit by another manufacture but nothing as of yet.<P>The second Ferrari that replaced the blown up Daytona was a real Testarossa (sp?). <P>The 308 in Magnum P.I. was real.||||reg||11
Z||000005||mrfixit58||12-06-1999||04:08 PM||||The Daytona in Miami Vice was a kit from a 70's vintage Corvette. The company that produced the kit is no longer in business. The molds have been sold and there have been a few attemps to re-offer this kit by another manufacture but nothing as of yet.<P>The second Ferrari that replaced the blown up Daytona was a real Testarossa (sp?). <P>The 308 in Magnum P.I. was real.||||reg||11
Z||000006||Fiero SE||12-06-1999||07:37 PM||||Yea, I would say a vette chassis. You know the Ferrari 355 Spider on the movie <BR>"The Rock". Well the one that was wrecked was a Ferrari Replica on a Fiero chassis. I want to take a fiero and either build a Lamborghini Diablo SE replica and stretch the frame 11" or a Ferrari 355 spider replica and stretch it 3" or 5" . Can't remember. ||||reg||4
Z||000007||Tom88gt||12-28-1999||02:28 PM||||My 2 cents.....<P>I agree, Magnum's 308 was real.  I watched the show a lot and back then I don't think there were too many kits on the market.  Plus the interior was correct.  Kit interiors are much newer.<P>The Miami Vice Daytona was a McBurmie (spelling???) kit built on a C3 Vette.  I believe the older C3s interior is somewhat similar to the Daytonas but I haven't compared the two.  Ferrari gave the show a real Testarossa but they had to dispose of the fake (what a scene!!! but what a shame also).  The stunt Testarossa is acutally a DeTomaso Pantera (Italian/Ford hybrid sold by Linclon Mercury for ......less than $10K, originally).<P>About the Daytona....McBurmie went out of business.  He was successfully sued by Ferrari for...........trademark infringment.  He used the Ferrai logo in his ads.  His copy of the body was not 100% exact nor has Ferrai ever been able to successfully sue anyone for copying their bodies.  Courts have ruled that Ferrari's aerodynamic bodies and various body/side scoops have a direct impact upon the car's high speed stability and brake/engine cooling.  Therefore they are a functional trait, not cosmetic, and cannot be trademarked.  This is the story I've read on the Daytona.  And It's the only time I know that Ferrari ever won against a kit company.  <P>I'm sure this will lead to the Mera so read Jan Kit Car.  The death of the Fireo ended the Mera and the lawsuit was dropped.  So we will never know if Ferrari would have won in court. <P>The McBurmie molds were sold (probably to pay legal expences) and are still in use by another kit company, perhaps Trident, but I'm not sure.||||reg||1
Z||000008||ChadMan||12-28-1999||03:32 PM||||What years were Meras built?  Were any Meras built with 88 underpinnings?||||reg||5
